Paragraph e_id": {
    "total_score": 0.0099859375,
    "has_citations": true,
    "text": "Specifying the scope of use of a dataset is an important step before sharing it [ 41]. Particularly, it is known that data-driven models are not reliable and may be unfair towards the groups that are not well represented in their (training) dataset [ 36,37]. Therefore, it is im- portant to identify the potential groups that are under-represented \u2217This work was supported in part by the National Science Foundation, Grant No. 2348919 and 2107290. This work is licensed under the Creative Commons BY-NC-ND 4.0 International License. Visit https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ to view a copy of this license. For any use beyond those covered by this license, obtain permission by emailing info@vldb.org. Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to the VLDB Endowment. doi:10.14778/3718057.3718072and for which the models under-perform . However, data in practice often misses to include demographic information of entities. Even in cases where some demographic information are available, many others may be missing that is necessary for identifying representa- tion bias [ 38]. To further motivate this problem, let us consider the following example: Example 1. A data-sharing platform (e.g., Chicago Open Data Portal [2]) would like to responsibly specify the under-represented andunder-performing groups in their shared data in order to limit their scope of use. However, their datasets either do not include grouping attributes (such as race) or only contain some of those. Moreover, targeting a comprehensive audit, they do not want to limit their scope to a small set of predefined groups. Instead, they want to be proactive in their detection process, even for the groups they might have missed to consider. For some cases such as image datasets, when a small and prede- termined set of grouping attributes (e.g., gender ) are missing, one may be able to use techniques such as crowdsourcing to identify under-represented groups based on those attributes [ 31]. Our prob- lem, however, is that the grouping attributes are not predetermined. In other words, we consider an unknown unknown situation where not only do we lack access to the grouping attributes but we also do not know the groups of interest. To address this problem, we introduce the problem of mining the potential groups that are under-represented and under-performing, when the grouping attributes are unknown. We refer to this prob- lem as Minoria mining . To the best of our knowledge, this paper isthe first to introduce and study the Minoria mining problem . In simple terms, the main question we want to answer in this paper is, \u201care there regions in data-space, representing potential groups that are (a) under-represented, for which (b) the model under-performs ?\u201d For instance, aligned with Example 1, our experi- ments on a Crimes dataset (\u00a7 7.1.1) identified North-side Chicago as under-represented and under-performing. This information helps the model developers and data owners such as Chicago Open Data Portal to proactively identify a potential issue, decide if it is socially concerning, and limit the dataset\u2019s scope-of-use accordingly. Similar to any other data mining problem, multiple approaches may come to mind for addressing this problem. For example, one can adapt a clustering technique (such as k-means clustering [ 28] or subspace and projective clustering [ 24,29,32]) to cluster the data into potential groups. However, as we shall show in our experi- ments (\u00a7 7.1.2) such an approach fails to identify under-represented groups, simply because there are not enough instances from those groups in the dataset. This is a well-known drawback of the clus- tering methods, that their performance degrades substantially for",
    "citation_focus_text": "Specifying the scope of use of a dataset is an important step before sharing it [ 41]. Particularly, it is known that data-driven models are not reliable and may be unfair towards the groups that are not well represented in their (training) dataset [ 36,37]. Therefore, it is im- portant to identify the potential groups that are under-represented \u2217This work was supported in part by the National Science Foundation, Grant No. Publication rights licensed to the VLDB Endowment. However, data in practice often misses to include demographic information of entities. Even in cases where some demographic information are available, many others may be missing that is necessary for identifying representa- tion bias [ 38]. A data-sharing platform (e.g., Chicago Open Data Portal [2]) would like to responsibly specify the under-represented andunder-performing groups in their shared data in order to limi"
  },
