Parent: The Ray-sweeping algorithm is more efficient than the Warm-up Algorithm because it leverages an observation that allows for constant time updates of skew values, whereas the Warm-up algorithm's running time is dominated by step two (computing medians and identifying high-skew projections).

The Ray-sweeping algorithm maintains some aggregates while sweeping over A\ud835\udc5b\n2, which enables updating the skew values in constant time. This is a key difference from the Warm-up Algorithm where each vertex requires calculating the \ud835\udc60\ud835\udc58\ud835\udc52\ud835\udc64 value, which takes more time.

The Ray-sweeping algorithm is similar to the Warm-up Algorithm in terms of enumerating A\ud835\udc5b\n2 and identifying high-skew projections but differs by how it computes medians more efficiently. It does this by sweeping a ray counterclockwise from the x-axis to the y-axis, maintaining some aggregates while doing so which enables updating skew values in constant time.

The pseudocode for the Ray-sweeping algorithm is provided in Algorithm vee and can be found in Section \u00a75.2 of the technical report [16]. A visualization of this approach on a toy example is also provided in Figure 3. The complexity analysis shows that, unlike Warm-up, it has no longer dominated by step two (computing medians and identifying high-skew projections).

The time complexity of the Ray-sweeping algorithm is equal to the time taken for enumerating the \ud835\udc5b\n2-th level of the arrangement – i.e., \ud835\udc42(\ud835\udc5b4\n3log1+\ud835\udefc\ud835\udc5b).

The time complexity of Ray-sweeping is no longer dominated by step two, which means that the running time of the second part (computing medians) can be done in constant time. This makes it more efficient than the Warm-up Algorithm.
[all_together_subsections] parent=Minoria Mining in 2D sample=2 attempt=4/5
The Parent-child relationship between the two algorithms is that they both aim to identify high skew directions, but they differ in their approach and efficiency. The Warm-up algorithm enumerates all possible projections of a parent node onto each direction before calculating the skew values for these projections. This process can be quite time-consuming as it requires computing the mean, median, and standard deviation for every single point on the projection axis.

The Ray-sweeping algorithm is an improvement over the Warm-up Algorithm in terms of efficiency. It follows a similar three-step approach: 1) Enumerate all possible projections (or directions), 2) Compute medians, and 3) Identify high-skew projections. However, instead of enumerating every vertex and then calculating the skew value for each one individually, it sweeps a ray counterclockwise from the x-axis to the y-axis, maintaining some aggregates while doing so. This allows us to update the skew values in constant time as we can find the new median using pre-calculated values of the previous vertex and calculate its skew value in constant time.

The Ray-sweeping algorithm is more efficient than the Warm-up Algorithm because it leverages an observation that while moving between two neighboring median regions, the set of dual lines above/below the median changes by only one element . This allows for a faster computation of skew values and reduces the time complexity.

The Ray-sweeping algorithm is presented in detail in Section 5.2 of the technical report [16].
